'''aloft''' (a-lôft′), ''prep. phr.'' as ''adv.'' and ''prep.'' [< ME. ''aloft, a loft, o loft'' (acc.), ''alofte, a lofte, o lofte'' (dat.), in fuller form ''on the loft, on the lofte, inne the lofte'', < Icel. ''ā lopt'' (acc. of motion), ''ā lopti'' (dat. of position), on high, aloft, lit. in the air: ''ā''=AS. ''an, on'', ME. ''a, o, on'', in, on, to; ''lopt'' (pron. ''loft'')=AS. ''lyft'', ME. ''lyft, luft, lift'' (E. ''lift''), the air, the sky, upper floor, loft: see ''loft'' and ''lift''<sup>1</sup>, the air.] '''I.''' ''adv.'' '''1.''' On high; in or into the air; high above the ground: as, the eagle soars ''aloft.''
{{smaller block|<poem>Then will I raise ''aloft'' the milk-white rose
With whose sweet smell the air shall be perfum’d.</poem>{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. VI., i. 1.}}}}
'''2.''' ''Naut.'', in or into the top; at the masthead, or on the higher yards or rigging; hence, on the upper part, as of a building.
{{smaller block|<poem>There’s a sweet little cherub that sits up ''aloft'',
To keep watch for the life of poor Jack.</poem>{{right|''Dibdin'', Poor Jack.}}}}
'''II.'''† ''prep.'' On the top or surface of; above.
{{smaller block|<poem>Now I breathe again
''Aloft'' the flood.</poem>{{right|''Shak.'', K. John, iv. 2.}}}}

